# 1. Introduction: Isomerism in MOFs and position of functional groups

The chemistry of metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) has been intensively expanded to include various research fields and related applications [1–4]. Their structural diversity, permanent porosity, low density, controllable stability, and the nature of their metal–ligand coordinations make these organic–inorganic materials an emerging platform for various applications, including gas separation, molecular storage, and catalysis [5–9]. The most important features of MOFs, which other porous materials lack, are the structural diversity and functionalization of both their inorganic and organic portions.

These features allow both portions of the framework to be independently controlled to give various MOF materials. For example, the same inorganic node (e.g., a \(Z\mathsf{n}_{4}0\) cluster) could bind with various ligands at different connecting angles to produce a series of related MOFs. Similarly, the same organic ligand (e.g., benzene-1,4-dicarboxylic acid, BDC) could form a variety of MOFs with different metal nodes. Furthermore, the isoreticular synthesis of MOFs has been developed, which produces MOFs that exhibit the same topology and connectivity but possess ligands of different lengths or varying functional groups [10].

MOFs also demonstrate their structural diversity through isomerism [11], where the same combination of metal salts and ligands could produce different MOFs under specific conditions. In the early stages of MOF studies, interpenetrated (or catenated) MOFs were reported as structural isomers of noninterpenetrated, original MOFs. In addition, slight changes in the coordination bonds or ligands could generate conformational changes in the MOF structures. Since these structures would have the same metal-based secondary building units (SBUs) and ligands with the same molecular formula, their relationship could be defined as structural isomers. In 2011, Zhou et al. classified the term ''framework isomers'' to explain the isomerism of MOFs [12]. In their studies, structural isomers of MOFs were identified as ''interpenetrated isomers'', ''conformational isomers'', and ''oriented isomers''. These terms are a significant contribution to MOF chemistry, as isomers within a series could show completely different properties and behaviors from one another. Therefore, careful structural analysis and definitions of the different isomers are remarkably important.

A different perspective has resulted in another new concept for ligand isomerism in MOFs. If the same metal-based MOFs possess the same ligands, but with different functional group positions, they would have the same molecular formula, framework, and structure, excluding the positions of the functional groups. For example, \(Z\mathrm{n}_{2}(\mathrm{BDC}{-}2,3{-}\mathrm{Cl}_{2})_{2}(\mathrm{d}a\mathrm{b}c\mathrm{o})\) and \(Z\mathrm{n}_{2}(\mathrm{BDC}{-}2,5{-}\mathrm{Cl}_{2})_{2}(\mathrm{d}a\mathrm{b}c\mathrm{o})\) (dabco \(=\) 1,4-diazabicyclo[2.2.2]octane) have the same structural features, but their chloro functional groups are in different positions. This class of MOFs could be additionally defined as ''functional group isomers''. MOFs could be prepared with multiple functional groups on a single ligand; for example, a single BDC ligand for MOF-5 synthesis could have a maximum of four chemical tags. Therefore, MOFs with more than two functional groups should be carefully investigated regarding possible ''functional group isomerism'' (Scheme 1.).

In this review, we have summarized MOFs with two or more functional groups on a single ligand component, and mostly

focused on the BDC type ligands. Investigations into the synthesis of MOFs with multiple functional groups, as well as their structural features and direct applications, have also been described herein. Ortho- and para-isomers were especially discussed because these two functional group positions can sometimes provide structures with different structural flexibilities and gas adsorption properties [13]. Moreover, we explore the structural changes that have been observed in functional group MOF isomers. It should be noted that there are many studies on multi-functionalized MOFs and their applications, and a number of reviews detailing this feature of MOFs that were produced by mixed-ligand strategies have already been published [14–18]; therefore, this review does not primarily focus the multi-functionalization of MOFs for applications.

