# 2. Two-identical functional groups in MOFs

# 2.1. Ether and alcohol functionalities

MOFs bearing two, alkyl ether side-chains as linkers are known to feature structural flexibility due to the solvent-like properties of ether functional groups. In 2010, Fischer et al. reported unexpected structural flexibility and gas adsorption exhibited by three MOFs: MOF-5, IRMOF-1, and \([\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{BDC})_{2}(\mathrm{dabco})]_{\mathrm{n}}\) , which possessed additional alkyl ether groups [19]. Additionally, \([\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{fu-BDC})_{2}(\mathrm{dabco})]_{\mathrm{n}}\) (fu-BDC \(=\) functionalized BDC-type linker) has either a narrow-pore \((n p)\) or large-pore \((l p)\) form depending on the presence or absence of guest molecules, and it undergoes a phase transition upon \(C0_{2}\) adsorption [20].

Fischer et al. also demonstrated a systematic approach for implementing and tuning the network dynamics and responsiveness of \([\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{fu-BDC})_{2}(\mathrm{dabco})]_{\mathrm{n}}\) MOFs by controlling the substituted functional groups with regioisomerism (Fig. 1a) [21]. A library of fu-BDC ligands (Fig. 1b) with a core benzene moiety bearing various side groups at different substituted positions were used to construct various \([\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{fu-BDC})_{2}(\mathrm{dabco})]_{\mathrm{n}}\) MOFs (1–9). The flexibility of MOFs constructed by two structural isomers (1 and 2 in Fig. 1b) was investigated. Substituents at positions 2 and 3 (ortho positioned) of the phenyl ring were oriented toward the same void, leading to congested cavities that arose from the substituents coming into contact. In comparison, the substituents were oriented toward two adjacent voids when they were located at positions 2 and 5 (para-positioned) of the phenyl group. Due to this configuration, 1 contains a uniform cavity environment, while 2 does not. As a result, 1 clearly shows a phase transition during desolvation and distinct, step-shape behavior upon \(C0_{2}\) adsorption, while no clear phase transition was observed in 2 (Fig. 1c).

In addition, MOFs with 2,5-disubstituted linkers bearing various simple alkoxy groups (1 and 3–7) or unsaturated substituents (8 and 9) were also systematically studied (Fig. 1b). The powder Xray diffraction (PXRD) patterns of dried samples of 1 and 3–9 revealed that a "breathing" transition occurred upon the removal of N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF) guest molecules (Fig. 1c). The effect of chain length on the flexibility of MOFs was investigated using functional groups 3, 4, 6, and 7. When all the MOFs bearing these groups were desiccated, the peaks in their PXRD profiles (over the range of \(7-10^{\circ}\)) were shifted to higher angles. As the chain length of the substituent increased, the degree of the shift decreased, which can be expected to decrease the structural flexibility (Fig. 1c).

A similar structural variation to the removal of the guest molecule was not observed in 7 because the linear optoxy substituents are too large to allow framework shrinkage (Fig. 1c). Hence, the structure remains intact in the \(l p\) form irrespective of the presence of DMF solvents. The lowest angular reflection \((2\theta=8.08^{\circ})\) in assynthesized 9 was that of prop-2-ynyloxy, which drastically shifted to \(2\theta=10.87^{\circ}\) when 9 was dried (Fig. 1c). These results can be interpreted as the result of space-saving hole packing through attractive interactions. They include van der Waals, bipolar, \(\pi{-}\pi\) , or hydrogen-bonding, between neighboring flexible substituents and/or between flexible substituted groups and the framework backbone.

