MOFs bearing alkene-containing dialkyloxy groups with 2,5- positional installation were also utilized for Pd detection in the solid state. Helal and co-workers successfully prepared Eu-based MOFs with dialkyenyl-functionalizations and ether linkages in 2019 (Fig. 3c) [25]. The coordination of palladium to the alkenes was monitored with UV–Vis spectroscopy and fluorescence microscopy. Although the pristine Eu-MOF showed bands at 252 and \(336~\mathrm{nm}\) in its UV–Vis spectrum, the external coordination of Pd to the alkene in the ligand showed an additional band at \(276~\mathrm{nm}\) . In addition, the color of the MOF crystals changed from orange to colorless when the framework sensed \(\mathrm{Pd}^{2+}\) ions in the solution. The selectivity of this dialkoxy-functionalized system to palladium ions was higher than its selectivity to other transition metals.

The dialkene functionalities at the terminal (2,5) positions of a ligand (i.e., para-functionalization) were also successfully utilized to form polymeric connections in MOFs. In 2019, the Cohen group reported polyMOF systems composed of divinyl-functionalized BDC ligands, where the vinyl and BDC groups were connected via 2,5-dialkoxy linkages [26]. Moreover, this system showed an interesting selectivity for polymerization. Although 2,5-divinyl-functionalized MOFs were synthesized under solvothermal conditions with polymerized BDC ligands, when BDC was polymerized with diene-based polyethylene glycol (PEG), random block polymers were obtained. The same polymerization with monoene-based PEG or cyclooctadiene produced block co-polymers and random co-monomers, respectively. It has been reported that the proportion of BDC in the polymer and the co-polymer directly affect MOFs. This polyMOF preparation with 2,5-divinyl functionalities was studied in Zr-based UiO-66 (UiO = University of Oslo) and IRMOF systems.

The molecular separation was studied with two methoxyfunctionalized, regioisomeric, NbO-type ZJNU MOFs (ZJNU = Zhejiang Normal University) [27]. In this case, ZJNU-58 and ZJNU-59 could be classified to skeletal isomerization, however, at the same time, the position of methoxy group toward isophthalate was controlled. In this study, ZJNU-58 and ZJNU-59 were synthesized under solvothermal conditions with a copper source and regioisomeric ligands. A 1,5-dimethoxynaphthalene moiety was used for ZJNU-58 synthesis and a 2,6-dimethoxynaphthalene-based ligand was utilized for preparing ZJNU-59 (Fig. 4). Interestingly, ZJNU-59 generally showed higher ethylene, carbon dioxide, and methane uptakes than those of regioisomeric ZJNU-58. Specifically, ethylene is one of the highest-value olefins used for commercial polymer production. It has been suggested that the property differences between regioisomers can lead to changes in the MOF surface area and pore size, which can improve the purification ability of valuable chemicals, such as ethylene.

