The effect of the hydroxy group was also investigated in a series of Mg-based MOFs in 2019[31]. The Zhai group combined a triangular, tripyridine ligand with an \(\left[\mathrm{Mg}_{3}(\mathrm{OH})(\mathrm{COO})_{6}\right]\) SBU to form MOFs with BDC, BDC-OH, and BDC-25- \(\left(\mathrm{OH}\right)_{2}\) moieties. The excited-state intramolecular proton transfer (ESIPT) was carefully studied, where the MOF functional groups produced a proton donor (OH group) and a proton acceptor (carbonyl group) simultaneously. Therefore, fluorescence sensing, especially for \(\mathrm{Al}^{3+}\), could be controlled by implementing intramolecular hydrogen bonds between adjacent hydroxy and carbonyl groups. In cases with more than one hydroxy group (e.g., one in both the 2 and 5 positions), the emission was enhanced due to the solvent effect and the fluorescence was decreased when more \(\mathrm{Al}^{3+}\) ions were added into the solution. Therefore, the number of hydroxy groups is quite important in this strategy. When compared with 16 other MOFs containing common metal atoms, such as \(\mathrm{Ga}^{3+}\), \(\mathrm{In}^{3+}\), \(\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}\), \(\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}\), \(\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}\), and \(\mathrm{Mg}^{2+}\), these 2,5-dihydroxy-functionalized Mg-MOFs exhibited the lowest \(\mathrm{Al}^{3+}\) detection limits and superior selectivity to those of the other MOFs.

# 2.2. Thiol and sulfide functionalities

In organic chemistry, thiol and sulfide functionalities are generally considered to be derivatives for alcohols and ethers, respectively. Therefore, thiol- and sulfur-functionalizations of MOFs

have also been significantly investigated, with thiol-containing MOFs in particular having been studied regarding their application in sorption and proton conductivity (Fig. 6) [32,33]. Che et al. prepared a ligand (L3) with thioether side chains \(\left(-\mathrm{SCH}_{2}\mathrm{SCH}_{2}\mathrm{CH}_{3}\right)\) at the 2,5-positions of BDC to afford a porous MOF, \(\left[\mathrm{Zn}_{4}0(\mathrm{L}3)_{3}\right]\), which was topologically equivalent to MOF-5 (Fig. 6) [32]. Thioethers with chemical softness serve as moderate electron donors and hydrophobic functional groups, which provide structural stability under moisture environments. The thioether-tethered \(\left[\mathrm{Zn}_{4}0(\mathrm{L}3)_{3}\right]\) MOF also showed significantly improved fluorescence intensity and metal-ion uptake capabilities

A thiol-functionalized UiO-66-type framework \(\mathrm{UiO-66-(SH)}_{2}\) was reported by Hong et al. [33], where the oxidation of the thiol groups in the MOF were completed with \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) and treated with an \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}\) solution to yield the respective, oxidized product bearing sulfonic acid \(\left(\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}\right)\) moieties, UiO-66- \(\left(\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}\right)_{2}\) (Fig. 7). Although, the positional control of thiol groups to ortho position was not mainly studied, the effect of thiol group was investigated. Since the sulfonate group could interrupt MOF formation due to its metal-coordinating ability, this post-synthetic modification (PSM) strategy, from thiol introduction to oxidation, is the most efficient way to introduce sulfonates into MOFs. The \(-\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}\) groups covalently bonded to the MOF backbone supplied the conduction channels with labile protons, and the MOF consequently exhibited a superprotonic conductivity of \(8.4 \times 10^{-2}\ \mathrm{S\ cm}^{-1}\) at \(80\ \mathrm{^\circ C}\) and \(90\%\) relative humidity. When the MOF solid was placed in hot water and stirred under reflux, the structure confirmed by PXRD was robust. The conductivity of this MOF is superior to that of any MOF conductors reported at that time.

In 2018, the Chen group prepared a series of functionalized UiO-66 MOFs, including 2,5-dithiol-functionalized UiO-66, and used them to evaluate the photocatalytic behavior of rhodamine B (RhB) degradation under visible light irradiation (Fig. 8) [34]. Isoreticular UiO-66, UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\), UiO-66-Br, UiO-66- \(\left(\mathrm{OH}\right)_{2}\), and UiO-66- \(\left(\mathrm{SH}\right)_{2}\) were prepared from commercial ligand sources, and their band gaps were measured by UV–Vis. Although the \(\mathrm{OH-}\) and \(\mathrm{NH}_{2^{-}}\) containing UiO-66 MOFs had narrow bandgaps and good visible light absorption, they could not effectively catalytically degrade organic dyes due to their conduction bands being close to the energy level of activated RhB. A broader band gap was observed for UiO-66- \(\left(\mathrm{SH}\right)_{2}\) compared to that of UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\). Therefore, UiO-66- \(\left(\mathrm{SH}\right)_{2}\) demonstrated enhanced photocatalytic reactivity, which was ascribed to its conduction band being lower than the energy level of activated RhB. The main contribution of this work is the new mechanism detailing the selective degradation of organic molecules with UiO-66 MOFs.

One year later, Wang and Li combined the three, aforementioned thiol- and sulfide-related works and conducted photocatalytic studies [35]. Pristine, thiol-functionalized UiO-66- \(\left(\mathrm{SH}\right)_{2}\) was prepared from direct solvothermal synthesis using BDC- \(\left(\mathrm{SH}\right)_{2}\) and sulfide-functionalized UiO-66- \(\left(\mathrm{SCH}_{3}\right)_{2}\) was obtained under solvothermal conditions with pre-synthesized BDC- \(\left(\mathrm{SCH}_{3}\right)_{2}\) ligands. Lastly, UiO-66- \(\left(\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}\right)_{2}\) was prepared following Hong's procedures [33]. A series of sulfur-functionalized UiO-66 s exhibited n-type semiconductor behavior, where the thiol, sulfide, and sulfonic acid functionalities played a key role in the bad gap and photocatalytic properties. Among them, disulfide-functionalized UiO-66- \(\left(\mathrm{SCH}_{3}\right)_{2}\) had a band gap narrower than that of the well-known UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) MOF. It also showed catalytic activity for water splitting that was superior to that of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) with a Pt co-catalyst.

