# 2.3. Amino and nitro functionalities

Amines are a key chemical handle in MOFs due to their accessibility and unique electronic characters. There are many examples

of amines undergoing various organic transformations to other functionalities. In 1999, an additional functionalization of MOFs using amino groups was intensively studied with isoreticular chemistry. The amino functionalizations of zinc-based IRMOF-3, zirconium-based UiO-66- \(\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) , and aluminum-based MIL- \(\cdot53{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) were all reported in the early stages of the MOF study. In addition, PSM strategies were significantly involved with introducing amino groups. These aminations in the solid state were the first PSMs of MOFs where amine functionalities were introduced [37].

Therefore, the number of amino groups and their positions on MOF ligands have been carefully studied with several different framework systems. In 2013, Rozes et al. systemically studied the effect of amino groups on Ti-based MIL-125 MOFs (Fig. 9) [38]. In
