Although ligands with para-positioned diamino and dinitro groups (e.g., \(\mathrm{BDC}{-}2{,}5{-}(\mathrm{NH}_{2})_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{BDC}{-}2{,}5{-}(\mathrm{NO}_{2})_{2}\) , respectively) have been prepared and studied for MOF formations and their applications, ligands with ortho-positioned diamino and dinitro groups (e.g., \(\mathrm{BDC}{-}2{,}3{-}(\mathrm{NH}_{2})_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{BDC}{-}2{,}3{-}(\mathrm{NO}_{2})_{2}\) ) have barely been reported in the literature. The preparation of ortho-dinitro and para-dinitro BDCs and their regioisomeric comparison were performed with a series of zinc-based dabelo MOFs (DMOFs) [42]. DMOFs have a bimetallic, paddle-wheel-type SBU, four BDC ligands, and two dabco pillar ligands. Interestingly, regioisomers \(\mathrm{DMOF}(\mathrm{Zn}){-}2{,}3{-}(\mathrm{NO}_{2})_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{DMOF}(\mathrm{Zn}){-}2{,}5{-}(\mathrm{NO}_{2})_{2}\) showed no significant differences in their physical properties (Fig. 11). In fact, their PXRD patterns and full \(\Nu_{2}\) isotherms were almost identical. From the numerous comparison studies that have been conducted on regioisomeric MOFs, a correlation between the electron density and structural flexibility of BDCs has been proposed. Additionally, highly electron-deficient dinitro-functionalized ligands showed no structural differences and flexibility upon evacuation. On the other hand, zinc-based DMOFs were not obtained from diamino ligands \((\mathrm{BDC}{-}2{,}3{-}(\mathrm{NH}_{2})_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{BDC}{-}2{,}5{-}(\mathrm{NH}_{2})_{2}\) ) and solvothermal conditions.

# 2.4. Alkyl and substituted alkyl functionalities

In the early stages of functional group control in MOFs, Horcajada et al. reported very important studies for functional group effects on the flexibility of MIL-88 MOFs [43]. Both BDC-based MIL-88B and BPDC-based MIL-88D were investigated with their type and number of functional groups being changed. Cl–, Br–, Me–, \(\mathsf{N H}_{2^{-}}\) , \(\mathsf{N O}_{2^{-}}\) , \(\left(\mathrm{OH}\right)_{2}-\) , \(\mathrm{F_{4}-}\) , and \(\left({\mathsf{C H}}_{4}\right)_{4}-\) moieties were installed on the BDC ligands and the subsequent MOF formation was evaluated and the MOF properties analyzed. Interestingly, the swelling property of MIL-88B decreased upon the insertion of more functional groups onto its BDC ligands (Fig. 12). Although monofunctionalized MIL-88-X showed flexibility and a large swelling property, almost-permanent porosity and a rigid structure were achieved by adding four functional groups to the BDC ligand (e.g., \(\mathrm{BDC}-(\mathrm{CH}_{3})_{4}\) ). This study clearly revealed an existent correlation between functional group and structural flexibility. Moreover, the steric hindrance of a ligand and the number of functional groups it bears can impact MOF structural features.

Functional group control was also studied with the alkyl groups of Zr-based MOFs. Zhou et al. designed a flexible PCN-700 series to realize switchable catalysis in \(C0_{2}\) cycloaddition reactions with epoxides [44,45]. The framework was subject to shrinkage along the \(c\) -axis upon expanding within the \(ab\) -plane. To tune the breathing amplitude, linkers with different substituents were selected: \(^{2,2^{\prime}}\) -dimethylbiphenyl- \(^{4,4^{\prime}}\) -dicarboxylic acid \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Me}_{2}\) -BPDC), \(^{2,2^{\prime},6,6^{\prime}}\) -tetramethylbiphenyl- \(^{4,4^{\prime}}\) -dicarboxylic acid \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Me}_{4}\) -BPDC), and 2-methylbiphenyl- \(^{4,4^{\prime}}\) -dicarboxylic acid \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Me}\) -BPDC)
