\(\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{D}_{2}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{b d p})\) structures [51]. Theses frameworks exhibited similar structural flexibilities to that of the parent MOF, \(\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{b d p})\), whose structure can be reversibly changed between collapsed and expanded phases by guest molecules (Fig. 16a). The collapsed phases demonstrate edge-to-face, \(\pi{-}\pi\) stackings between the aryl groups and the edge of the neighboring \(\mathsf{b d p}^{2-}\) ligands due to the perpendicular orientation between the aryl rings (Fig. 16a). However, in the case of \(\mathsf{C o}(p\mathrm{-}\mathsf{F}_{2}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{b d p})\), the dihedral angle between the aryl rings is only \(9.7^{\circ}\) and the \(\pi{-}\pi\) interactions are replaced by a \(\pi{-}\pi\) stacked configuration (Fig. 16b). This was ascribed to the electronegative fluorine groups decreasing the electronic disparity between the ring edge and face. As a result, the \(\pi{-}\pi\) stacked configuration in \(\mathsf{C o}(p\mathrm{-}\mathsf{F}_{2}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{b d p})\) is more favorable than the edge-to-face \(\pi{-}\pi\) forces in the framework. To examine the role of ligand variation in sorption-induced structural variations, high-pressure \(\mathsf{C H}_{4}\) adsorption measurements were conducted under pressures ranging from 1 bar to 70 bars at \(25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (Fig. 16c and d). The stepshifts of fluorine-functionalized MOFs moved to a lower pressure relative to that of \(\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{b d p})\), revealing that the introduction of fluorine groups destabilize the collapsed phase. When the structure undergoes a \(\mathsf{C H}_{4}\)-induced phase change, the four edge-to-face \(\pi{-}\pi\) interactions among the aryl rings in \(\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{b d p})\) must be broken. On the other hand, only two \(\pi{-}\pi\) stackings involving the central rings were broken during the phase change of \(\mathsf{C o}(p\mathrm{-}\mathsf{F}_{2}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{b d p})\). The number of broken \(\pi{-}\pi\) interactions was reduced, which is an essential factor in the destabilization of collapsed-phase, fluorine-functionalized MOFs. Additionally, the \(\mathsf{C H}_{4}\)-expanded structure of \(\mathsf{C o}(p\mathrm{-}\mathsf{F}_{2}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{b d p})\) was stabilized by weak \(\mathsf{C{-}H{\cdots}F{-}C}\) hydrogen-bonding interactions. This work demonstrated a design strategy for flexible MOFs that undergo structural phase changes upon gas adsorption.

