The electron density of benzene can be altered by substituting functional groups onto the ring, which affects the strength and length of the metal–carboxylate coordination bonds. Thanks to these electronic properties, the structural flexibility of MOFs with substituted ligands has been investigated [53,54]. The first study regarding the correlation between two functional groups and structural flexibility was reported by the Cohen group in 2011 [55]. In this study, dually functionalized amino-halo BDCs (BDC\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{X}\)) exhibiting region isomerism were prepared by halogenating BDC- \(\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) ligands. A total of five ligands (BDC-2,3-NH2Cl, BDC\(2,5\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}\), BDC-2,3- \(\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{Br}\), BDC-2,5- \(\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{Br}\), and BDC-2,5- \(\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{I}\)) were employed in both Zr-based UiO-66 and Zn-based DMOF syntheses. Although these five new, dually functionalized, UiO-66 MOFs showed predictable properties, the flexible DMOFs displayed very interesting phenomena upon regioisomerism. While the two ortho-functionalized DMOFs, DMOF-2,3- \(\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}\) and DMOF-2,3- \(\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{Br}\), showed highly porous structures after evacuation, the three para-functionalized DMOFs, DMOF-2,5- \(\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}\), DMOF-2,5- \(\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{Br}\), and DMOF-2,5- \(\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{I}\), displayed very small amounts of \(\Nu_{2}\) adsorption after evacuation.

For further investigation, Kim et al. designed BDC ligands substituted with various combinations of two different functional groups (pairs of \(\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\), -Cl, -OMe, and \(\mathrm{-NO}_{2}\)) and used them to synthesize a series of zinc(II)-based DMOFs (Fig. 17) [56]. Among this series of MOFs, those with \(\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\) and -Cl (L4a) or \(\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\) and -OMe (L8a)
