para-substituted ligands exhibited unique flexibility features in their \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) isotherms, which resulted from the electron-rich environment of the benzene ring. This work suggests that various combinations of substituted functional groups in ligands can provide electronically different environments, affecting structural flexibility. Therefore, not only should intermolecular interactions or framework-to-framework interactions between functional groups be carefully considered for designing new MOFs with chemical tags, but also the electronic properties of the functional groups.

A series of dually functionalized MOFs with two chemical tags underwent PSMs to enable their direct application [57]. Zn-based UMCM-1 (UMCM = University of Michigan crystalline material) MOFs were synthesized with amino-halo functionalized ligands. Interestingly, the ortho-functionalized UMCM-1- \([2,3-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}]\) and UMCM-1- \([2,5-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}]\) were not synthesized under the representative solvothermal conditions of UMCMs. The obtained para-functionalized UMCM-1- \([2,5-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{X}]\) \(\mathbf{\nabla}X=\mathbf{Cl}\), Br, and I) MOFs were investigated for PSM efficiency. Since the halogens have different sizes, the effect of pore size and substrate size could be compared in a PSM manner. It was clearly confirmed that the size of the reagent is more critical for PSM efficiency than the size of the MOF pores (Fig. 18).

Solid-state chlorination is another PSM approach for dually functionalized MOFs, which was achieved by DeCoste et al. in

2015 [58]. In this study, UiO-66- \(\cdot\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) was prepared and chlorinated in a PSM manner through electrophilic aromatic substitution with \(\mathsf{C l}_{2}\). It was proposed that both ortho-functionalized UiO-66–2,3- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}\) and para-functionalized UiO-66–2,5- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}\) were obtained, and neither dechlorination nor additional chlorination occurred. Moreover, the generated HCl could react with the zirconiumcarboxylate coordination bonds and form an ammonium chloride on the amino group.

In addition to this PSM study, the effect of metals on the regioisomerism of DMOFs with amino-halo groups was studied by the Kim group in 2019 [59]. Since DMOFs could be prepared with Co, Ni, Cu, and Zn metals, \(\mathrm{DMOF}(\mathrm{M})–2,3–\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}\) and \(\mathrm{DMOF}(\mathsf{M})–2,5-\) \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}\) ( \(\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{C}\mathbf{o}\), Cu, and Zn) were prepared and their structural flexibility for evacuation was investigated with experimental approaches and computational analysis. As reported early, Zn-based DMOF regioisomers showed significantly different \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) isotherms. However, the quantity of \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorbed by the Cu-based DMOFs was lower than that adsorbed by the other DMOFs. Moreover, both \(\mathrm{DMOF}(\mathrm{Co})–2,3–\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}\) and \(\mathrm{DMOF}(\mathrm{Co})–2,5–\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}\) showed almost identical full \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) isotherms upon evacuation. These results clearly suggested that the coordination bond between the metal (in the SBU) and carboxylate (in the BDC ligand) of DMOFs is highly related to the DMOF structural flexibility after evacuation. Furthermore, quantum chemical simulations supported these experimental findings. Both regioisomers of the Zn-based and Co-based DMOFs had low-ordered and highly ordered metal centers in their SBUs, respectively.

# 3.2. More than three-functional groups in MOFs

In 2017, Kim et al. synthesized DMOFs substituted with either two identical functionalities or three functional groups and investigated their structural flexibilities [42]. Although the structures of two regioisomeric MOFs \(\mathrm{DMOF}{-}2,5{-}(\mathrm{OMe})_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{DMOF}{-}2,3(\mathrm{OMe})_{2}\) were similar, as determined by their PXRD patterns (Fig. 19a), their \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) isotherms and Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) surface areas were significantly different. The \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) isotherms exhibited that the \(\mathrm{DMOF}{-}2,3{-}(\mathrm{OMe})_{2}\) was deemed highly porous with a BET surface area of \(1554\,\mathrm{~m}^{2}\,\mathrm{~g}^{-1}\), while \(\mathrm{DMOF}{-}2,5{-}(\mathrm{OMe})_{2}\) was practically non-porous with a surface area of \(45\,\mathrm{~m}^{2}\,\mathrm{~g}^{-1}\). This phenomenon was previously reported for two MOFs with different functional group substitutions: \(\mathrm{DMOF}{-}2,5{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}\) and \(\mathrm{DMOF}{-}2,5{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{OMe}\) [55,56].

Furthermore, MOFs with tri-functionalized ligands were synthesized by introducing an \(-\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) or \(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) group onto the benzene ring of BDC- \((\mathrm{OMe})_{2}\) ligands, where the different effects brought on by the electron-withdrawing or electron-donating groups were analyzed. The \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) isotherms were also collected for these MOFs to examine their structural flexibilities (Fig. 19b). Both DMOFs showed only minimal disparity in their porosities, even though they had completely different electronic environments. This work concludes that structure flexibility is affected by not only the electron density of the benzene ring, but also by the positions of the functional groups.

Electronically rich, dimethoxy-amino-functionalized ligands \((\mathrm{BDC}{-}2,5{-}(\mathrm{OMe})_{2}{-}3{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2})\) were also used for UiO-66 preparation and \(\mathrm{Xe/Kr}\) separation experiments by Kim and Bae in 2018 [60]. The electron density-controlled UiO-66 s were synthesized with various ligand functionalizations to give MOFs ranging from electronically deficient \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{F}_{4}\) to electronically rich UiO-66– \(2,5{-}(\mathrm{OMe})_{2}{-}3{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\). Since the \(\mathrm{Xe/Kr}\) separations require strong interactions between \(\mathrm{Xe/Kr}\) and the MOFs, the electron-rich ligands and polar-unsymmetrical functional groups enhanced the separation efficiency.
