# 4. Miscellaneous

4.1. Single functional group with positional controls

The position of a methyl group in V-shaped diisophthalate ligands was investigated by He and co-workers [61]. Three Cu-based MOFs (ZJNU-81, ZJNU-82, and ZJNU-83, Fig. 20) were successfully prepared and single crystal XRD confirmed the isoreticular structures of ZJNU-82 and ZJNU-83. However, ZJNU-81 showed a different framework with isomeric ligands and a copper cation. The steric factor of the methyl group played a key role during the self-assembly process for these MOFs.

In general, ZJNU-81 showed a surface area \((2720\;\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})\) higher than those of ZJNU-82 \((2231\;\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})\) and ZJNU-83 \((2015\;\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})\). The pore sizes of these three MOFs exhibited the same trend. During gas separation experiments, the \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C H_{4}}\) and \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C O_{2}}\) selectivities of each MOF were calculated by the IAST method, where the order of selectivity (highest to lowest) was as follows: ZJNU-81 > ZJNU-82 > ZJNU-83. This study provided very interesting and important insights into functional group positioning during MOF preparation.
Sun et al. reported 1D channel-type, Cd-based MOFs with two regioisomeric ligands: pyridinium-connected \(4{,}4^{\prime}\)-bis[(4-carboxypyridino)methyl]-biphenyl dichloride \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L}10\cdot\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\right)\) and \(1,1^{\prime}\)-bis(4-carboxybenzyl)-4,4'-bipyridinium dichloride \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L}11\cdot\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\right.\), Fig. 21) [62]. Generally, both ligands were prepared by connecting the biphenyl and benzoic acid components with methylene linkages. The pyridinium is located on the benzoic acid portion in L10 and the biphenyl portion in L11. Not surprisingly, both ligands displayed almost identical MOF structures (except for the charge distribution on their structures) after a solvothermal reaction with \(\mathsf{C d B r}_{2}\). Although their coordination linkages and packing geometries were very similar, their photo-responsive behaviors in the solid state were quite different. Under the same UV irradiation, MOFs made from L10 showed bright, yellow-green luminescence while MOFs prepared from L11 displayed no luminescence. Interestingly, the color of the MOF made from L11 changed to blue under irradiation with a xenon lamp, but its original color returned after it was stored under atmospheric conditions for several hours.

Wuttke et al. studied the positional controls of a single functionality using nitro groups [63]. In this study, the position of the nitro group was controlled (ortho- or meta-positions relative to the carboxylic acid moiety) in MIL-143 MOFs. Structural analyses were performed where the chemical/thermal stabilities of the regioisomeric MOFs, along with their application to isoreticular synthesis and mixed MOF preparations, were carefully investigated. In addition, much smaller positional modifications were achieved in triazole-based linker molecules for MOF preparation. In 2014, Li and co-workers reported Cd-based 1D channel-type MOFs prepared by combining BDC and bis-triazole ligands [64]. Specifically, two regioisomeric bis-triazoles were prepared and applied to MOF formation for detailed structural analysis. Although the above results are based on the positional controls of a single functionality, they provide information that can be applied to the design of molecular-storage and energy-related MOF materials.

# 4.2. Functional group mixtures in MOFs

A mixed-ligand strategy for MOFs has been considered for preparing multi-functional materials. By simply blending more than two different ligands during MOF formation, a solid-mixture could be obtained [14]. A maximum of eight functionalities was successfully incorporated into MOFs using this mixed-ligand strategy. Additionally, several methods were employed in attempts to control the structural features and ligand positions of these MOFs [65]. Since there are many informative review articles about mixed MOFs in the literature [15–18], the preparation and application of simple mixed MOFs is not discussed here. Rather, we focus on solid mixtures of more than two target functionalities when discussing regioisomerism.

Two chemical tags, amino and bromo groups, were mixed together and installed into UiO-66 MOFs via a dual PSM strategy. In 2011, Kim et al. reported the mixed MOF UiO-66- \(\cdot(\mathrm{Br})(\mathrm{NH}_{2})\), where the ratio of Br to \(\mathsf{N H}_{2}\) could be controlled [66]. The homogeneous mixture of the two different ligands in the solid-state was investigated with aerosol time-of-flight mass spectrometry (ATOFMS), and the ratio of each ligand was determined by nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) after acid digestion. Moreover, each chemical tag was modified to target specific functionalities. Specifically, the amino group was converted into an amide by acylation and the bromo group was transformed to a cyano group by cyanation. Both of these transformations were achieved via solid-state PSMs. The efficiency of PSM was not affected by the ligand-mixing ratio or the sequential order of the PSMs. The porosity and gas-adsorption properties of UiO-66 MOFs were also investigated and controlled with mixtures of \(\mathsf{B D C-N H}_{2}\) and \(\mathsf{B D C-N O}_{2}\) ligands (Fig. 22) [67]. Solid mixtures of UiO-66 MOFs with different

ligand ratios were synthesized and analyzed for gas adsorptions. As expected, the MOF \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) and \(\mathsf{C H}_{4}\) adsorptions were enhanced when increasing the molar ratio of \(\mathsf{B D C-N H}_{2}\) into UiO-66- \(\cdot\mathrm{NO}_{2}\).

In 2013, the Stock research team reported high-throughput screening results for a Cr-based MIL-101 synthesis with various functionalized BDCs; namely, amino-, nitro-, sulfonate-, hydroxy-, methyl-, carboxylate-, bromo-, chloro-, fluoro-, and iodo-functionalizations (Fig. 23) [68]. Since traditional, Cr-based MIL-101 synthesis required high temperatures and HF conditions, any variation in this synthesis is relatively restricted compared to those of other chemically stable MOFs, such as UiO-66 and PCN series. After extensive screening for synthetic conditions, it was revealed that the source of chromium metal is critical for these syntheses. Moreover, some undesired side reactions were observed during the solvothermal synthesis, such as decarboxylation and chlorination. To prepare a dually functionalized MIL-101, a Cr-based MIL-101-Br- \(\cdot\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) MOF was first constructed, and the nitro group was then reduced into an amino group via a PSM. In the previous study, the high reactivity and low thermal stability of the amino group made it the most difficult chemical tag to install into MIL-101 structures.

Around the same time, Reinsch et al. conducted a high-throughput screening of Al-based CAU-10 (CAU = Christian-Albrechts-University) MOFs and their mixed-ligand systems (Fig. 24) [69]. BDC-Br and BDC-ligand mixtures were used for preparing CAU-10-H/Br MOFs, while \(\mathsf{B D C-N H}_{2}\) and \(\mathsf{B D C-N O}_{2}\) mixtures were employed to construct \(\mathsf{C A U-10-N O_{2}/N H_{2}}\) MOFs. The formation of these MOFs was confirmed by PXRD, and \({}^{1}\mathrm{H}\) NMR experiments analyzed the ratios of the ligands. Interestingly, in situ formylation seemed to have occurred; therefore, \([\mathrm{Al(OH)(BDC-NO_{2})_{0.55}(BDC-N H_{2})_{0.23}(B D C-N H C H O)_{0.22}}]\) was determined as the structure for the mixed CAU-10 MOFs. The addition of NH2 to CAU-10- \(\cdot\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) had no effect on the accessibility of the pores. However, the sorption capabilities of the MOFs for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) and \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) were increased.

Generally, mixed- \(\cdot\mathrm{Hg}\) and strategies have been adopted to secure more empty space with non-functionalized ligands in MOF pores for molecular storage, catalysis, and sensing. In addition, the blending of two functionalized ligands was also investigated for several systems to achieve dual PSMs, especially for molecular interactions. In our opinion, these mixed-ligand strategies displayed predictable properties and results when applied to regioisomeric systems.

# 5. Conclusions and future outlooks

MOFs with high surface areas and porosities are a new class of porous, crystalline solids that can be used in a variety of applications, such as gas adsorption and separation, catalytic activity, and conductivity. Additionally, these MOFs can alter luminescent characteristics through their library of applicable metal components and organic linkers. To examine the individual effects of various functional groups on an organic ligand, the substituents must be properly positioned. In this context, regioisomerism is beneficial for locating the functional groups of the ligands in a MOF system and introducing suitable structural and functional traits into a pristine framework. This provides an effective route towards controlling MOF pore sizes and enhancing desirable surface properties in these pores. The incorporation of regioisomeric ligands into a MOF triggers structural flexibility, intriguing adsorption behavior, and specific photo or thermal responses.

